<div class="hub-intro" style="padding-top:2rem"><p>For athletes, strength matters because of what it does for speed, jumping and durability. The best single measure is relative strength: how much you squat compared with what you weigh.</p><p>A back squat of 1.5 times bodyweight is a common target for high school athletes, and 2 times bodyweight is strong for a college athlete. Research consistently links higher relative squat strength to faster sprints and higher jumps, though the carryover shrinks once an athlete is already very strong. Past that point, power and speed work usually pay off more than adding weight to the bar.</p></div>
